column1:
8:12|Were they ashamed of the detestable things they did?
They felt no shame at all;
they did not know how to blush.
So they will fall among the fallen;
when I call them to account, they will stumble,”
says the **LORD**.|Keeps shame questions and fall/stumble imagery; renders visitation as accountability.

10:10|But the **LORD** is the true God;
he is the living God and everlasting King.
The earth trembles at his anger;
the nations cannot endure his fury.|Retains contrast and distinct divine titles.

10:14|Everyone is senseless, without knowledge;
every goldsmith is shamed by the carved image.
The image he casts is a lie;
there is no breath in them.|Keeps universal human limitation, craftworker shame, and absent breath.

12:3|But you, **LORD**, know me;
you see me and test my heart toward you.
Drag them away like sheep for slaughter;
set them apart for the day of killing.|Preserves violent petition and consecration verb’s troubling use.

15:2|When they ask you, ‘Where shall we go?’ tell them: This is what the **LORD** says:
Those destined for death, to death;
those for the sword, to the sword;
those for famine, to famine;
those for captivity, to captivity.|Preserves four destinies and repeated relentless formula.

15:9|The mother of seven languishes;
she breathes out her life.
Her sun sets while it is still day;
she is ashamed and humiliated.
I will hand their survivors over to the sword
before their enemies,” declares the **LORD**.|Retains seven, dying/daylight image, and source shift to survivors.

18:21|So give their children over to famine;
pour them out to the power of the sword.
Let their wives be childless widows;
let their men be struck down by death,
their young men by the sword in battle.|Keeps entire harsh imprecation and distinct family victims without blessing or censoring it.
